        "title": "03 - Transport Phenomena",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Transport phenomena (BSL after Bird, Stewart & Lightfoot) unifies the three transport modes.\nThis reveals deep analogies that let you transfer correlations across domains.\nThe BSL unification has a structural parallel in distributed systems: throughput, latency, and packet loss are three coupled transport phenomena in a network, all governed by queue dynamics and driving forces (demand gradients). The Peclet number (advection/diffusion ratio) maps to bandwidth-dominated vs latency-dominated regimes — high Pe means the bulk flow matters more than local mixing, just as high-bandwidth links mean propagation delay dominates over queuing delay."
    }
